                passageText = "In contemporary Nigeria, the pervasive discourse surrounding agricultural productivity frequently fixates almost exclusively upon boosting raw harvest yields through improved seed varieties, mechanization, and chemical fertilizers. While elevating agronomic output is undeniably crucial, this narrow emphasis obscures an equally devastating reality: post-harvest losses. National agricultural surveys estimate that between thirty and fifty percent of all perishable crops—particularly horticultural produce like tomatoes, peppers, and citrus fruits, as well as root tubers like yams and cassava—rot before ever reaching consumer retail markets.\n\nThe architectural root of this colossal waste lies in the near-total absence of an integrated cold-chain logistics network, dilapidated rural feeder roads, and archaic storage facilities. Rural farmers, lacking refrigerated transport or solar-powered cold storage hubs, are routinely forced into distress sales during harvest peaks, selling their produce at giveaway prices to predatory aggregators to avert total spoilage. Weeks later, urban consumers face exorbitant market prices as scarcity sets in. Investing strategically in rural agro-processing hubs and temperature-controlled storage would stabilize agricultural commodity prices, augment smallholder income, conserve millions of tons of food, and drastically curtail the nation's reliance on costly food import bills.",
                difficulty = "Medium",
