                passageText = "As the international community coalesces around ambitious decarbonization mandates to arrest catastrophic planetary warming, developing nations in Sub-Saharan Africa confront a profound existential paradox: balancing global climate responsibilities against the urgent moral imperative to eradicate pervasive energy poverty. Currently, over six hundred million people in Sub-Saharan Africa lack reliable access to electricity. This chronic deficit cripples industrial manufacturing, paralyzes public healthcare delivery, and forces millions of households to rely upon polluting biomass fuels for basic cooking, resulting in hundreds of thousands of avoidable premature deaths annually from indoor air pollution.\n\nWestern multilateral financial institutions have increasingly instituted stringent prohibitions against financing fossil fuel infrastructure—including transitional natural gas projects—in developing economies. African leaders and economists vigorously contest this blanket moratorium as hypocritical and economically asphyxiating. Natural gas, they contend, emits significantly lower carbon dioxide than coal and heavy fuel oil, serving as an indispensable baseload stabilizer to support intermittent renewable sources like solar photovoltaic and wind power. A genuinely 'just' energy transition cannot demand that the world's least emitting continent—responsible for less than four percent of cumulative historical greenhouse gas emissions—forego the industrialization required to lift its burgeoning population out of poverty. Equitable climate justice demands substantial climate finance, technology transfer, and flexibility to utilize domestic gas reserves while aggressively scaling solar and hydro capacity.",
